WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.211
Structured analytic techniques are repeatable ways to improve reasoning.

00:00:03.211 --> 00:00:07.706
They do not guarantee the correct answer, and they do not replace expertise.

00:00:07.706 --> 00:00:12.200
Their value is that they force analysts to slow down in useful places,

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:13.583
challenge assumptions, consider alternatives,

00:00:13.583 --> 00:00:16.348
and identify evidence that would change the assessment.

00:00:16.348 --> 00:00:19.114
They are practical guardrails against common reasoning errors.

00:00:19.114 --> 00:00:21.880
A key assumptions check is one simple technique.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:26.719
List the assumptions that support the assessment, then ask which ones are necessary, weak,

00:00:26.719 --> 00:00:27.757
untested, or risky.

00:00:27.757 --> 00:00:31.905
For example, an incident assessment may assume that logging coverage is complete,

00:00:31.905 --> 00:00:34.325
that an alert rule behaved as expected,

00:00:34.325 --> 00:00:37.436
or that a change ticket accurately describes what happened.

00:00:37.436 --> 00:00:40.893
By naming assumptions, the analyst can decide what needs validation.

00:00:40.893 --> 00:00:43.313
Indicators and warnings help analysts think forward.

00:00:43.313 --> 00:00:45.388
Instead of only asking what happened,

00:00:45.388 --> 00:00:49.536
ask what observable signs would suggest that a situation is improving, worsening,

00:00:49.536 --> 00:00:50.573
or changing direction.

00:00:50.573 --> 00:00:54.376
In a defensive security context, indicators might include repeated alert patterns,

00:00:54.376 --> 00:00:58.524
changes in error rates, unusual access to sensitive systems, new vendor guidance,

00:00:58.524 --> 00:01:00.253
or completion of remediation milestones.

00:01:00.253 --> 00:01:03.364
Indicators should be observable and connected to the question.

00:01:03.364 --> 00:01:05.784
Analysis of competing hypotheses helps test alternatives.

00:01:05.784 --> 00:01:09.933
Start with several plausible explanations, then compare the evidence against each one.

00:01:09.933 --> 00:01:13.044
The point is not to defend a favorite answer.

00:01:13.044 --> 00:01:17.193
The point is to ask which explanation best fits the evidence and

00:01:17.193 --> 00:01:20.650
which evidence would be expected if each hypothesis were true.

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:25.144
This can reveal when an early conclusion rests on weak or selective evidence.

00:01:25.144 --> 00:01:28.947
Red team review, premortem, and alternative futures exercises can also help.

00:01:28.947 --> 00:01:32.404
A red team review asks someone to challenge the reasoning.

00:01:32.404 --> 00:01:37.935
A premortem asks how the assessment could fail and what signs would have warned the team.

00:01:37.935 --> 00:01:42.083
Alternative futures ask what different paths are plausible if key drivers change.

00:01:42.083 --> 00:01:46.578
These techniques are especially useful when decisions are important and uncertainty is high.

00:01:46.578 --> 00:01:47.960
Use these tools practically.

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:52.109
A short assumptions check may be enough for a daily operational note.

00:01:52.109 --> 00:01:57.640
A more formal competing hypotheses matrix may be appropriate for a complex investigation or strategic assessment.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:59.714
The purpose is not academic ritual.

00:01:59.714 --> 00:02:02.134
The purpose is better reasoning, clearer uncertainty,

00:02:02.134 --> 00:02:04.900
and analysis that can stand up to review.
